{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E4Zv6ZbHsvX2RGkgHuNnNx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: LSP Visual Documentation with Playwright Demos\n2\t\n3\t**Date**: 2026-04-06\n4\t**Branch**: `lsp-visual-docs`\n5\t**Script**: `scripts/run-lsp-visual-docs-loop.sh`\n6\t\n7\t## Design Principle\n8\t\n9\t**Show, don't tell.** Each LSP feature gets a motivating real-world scenario that a data engineer would actually encounter, automated via Playwright against VS Code Server (code-server) to produce screenshots and screen recordings. Documentation is generated as markdown with embedded media, suitable for the project README, docs site, or GitHub wiki.\n10\t\n11\t## Demo Infrastructure\n12\t\n13\t**Approach**: Playwright drives a headless (or headed) code-server instance with the smelt extension installed. Each demo script:\n14\t1. Opens a purpose-built example workspace with a compelling scenario\n15\t2. Performs editor interactions (typing, hovering, clicking, Ctrl+click, etc.)\n16\t3. Captures annotated screenshots and/or short video clips\n17\t4. Outputs media to `docs/demos/media/`\n18\t\n19\t**Why code-server**: It's VS Code in a browser — Playwright's native territory. No Electron hacks, no flaky desktop automation. The smelt extension loads identically since it's a standard LSP client.\n20\t\n21\t**Demo workspace**: `examples/demo_workspace/` — a small but realistic analytics pipeline designed so each feature has a natural, compelling demonstration point.\n22\t\n23\t## Demo Workspace Design\n24\t\n25\tThe workspace tells a story: a startup's analytics pipeline with users, events, and revenue.\n26\t\n27\t```\n28\texamples/demo_workspace/\n29\t├── smelt.yml\n30\t├── sources.yml                    # raw data sources with typed columns\n31\t├── models/\n32\t│   ├── staging/\n33\t│   │   ├── stg_users.sql          # clean user data from source\n34\t│   │   └── stg_events.sql         # clean event data from source  \n35\t│   ├── intermediate/\n36\t│   │   ├── user_sessions.sql      # sessionized events (refs stg_events)\n37\t│   │   └── user_first_purchase.sql # first purchase per user (refs stg_events, stg_users)\n38\t│   └── marts/\n39\t│       ├── user_lifetime_value.sql # LTV calculation (refs multiple intermediates)\n40\t│       └── daily_revenue.sql       # daily revenue rollup\n41\t└── models_broken/                  # copies with intentional errors for demo\n42\t    ├── bad_ref.sql                 # typo in smelt.ref() → diagnostics demo\n43\t    ├── type_mismatch.sql           # VARCHAR vs INTEGER comparison → type error demo\n44\t    └── missing_column.sql          # references non-existent column → undeclared column demo\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t## Status Key\n48\t\n49\t- `[ ]` — Not started\n50\t- `[~]` — In progress\n51\t- `[x]` — Complete\n52\t- `[!]` — Blocked or needs review\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## Phase 0: Demo Infrastructure Setup `[~]`\n57\t\n58\t**Goal**: Set up Playwright, code-server, and the demo workspace so all subsequent phases can focus purely on scripting demos.\n59\t\n60\t**Work items**:\n61\t- [ ] Create `docs/demos/` directory structure:\n62\t  ```\n63\t  docs/demos/\n64\t  ├── package.json              # Playwright + dependencies\n65\t  ├── playwright.config.ts      # code-server base URL, timeouts, video settings\n66\t  ├── helpers/\n67\t  │   ├── code-server.ts        # Launch/teardown code-server with smelt extension\n68\t  │   ├── editor.ts             # High-level helpers: openFile(), typeText(), hover(), triggerCompletion(), goToDefinition(), etc.\n69\t  │   └── capture.ts            # Screenshot/video capture with annotation overlays\n70\t  ├── scripts/\n71\t  │   └── start-code-server.sh  # Launch code-server pointing at demo workspace\n72\t  ├── tests/                    # Playwright test files (one per feature)\n73\t  ├── media/                    # Generated screenshots and videos\n74\t  └── output/                   # Generated markdown documentation\n75\t  ```\n76\t- [ ] Create `examples/demo_workspace/` with `smelt.yml` and `sources.yml`\n77\t- [ ] Write `sources.yml` with a realistic schema:\n78\t  ```yaml\n79\t  sources:\n80\t    raw:\n81\t      tables:\n82\t        users:\n83\t          columns:\n84\t            - name: user_id\n85\t              type: INTEGER\n86\t            - name: email\n87\t              type: VARCHAR\n88\t            - name: signup_date\n89\t              type: DATE\n90\t            - name: plan_type\n91\t              type: VARCHAR\n92\t        events:\n93\t          columns:\n94\t            - name: event_id\n95\t              type: INTEGER\n96\t            - name: user_id\n97\t              type: INTEGER\n98\t            - name: event_type\n99\t              type: VARCHAR\n100\t            - name: event_time\n101\t              type: TIMESTAMP\n102\t            - name: revenue_cents\n103\t              type: INTEGER\n104\t  ```\n105\t- [ ] Write all model SQL files (staging, intermediate, marts) — keep them short (5-15 lines each) but realistic\n106\t- [ ] Write the `models_broken/` variants with intentional errors\n107\t- [ ] Install Playwright: `npm init -y && npm install @playwright/test playwright`\n108\t- [ ] Write `helpers/code-server.ts` — launches code-server with `--install-extension` for the smelt VSIX, returns the base URL\n109\t- [ ] Write `helpers/editor.ts` — wraps common VS Code web interactions:\n110\t  - `openFile(page, path)` — via quick-open (Ctrl+P)\n111\t  - `goToLine(page, line)` — via Ctrl+G\n112\t  - `hoverWord(page, word)` — position cursor and wait for hover widget\n113\t  - `triggerCompletion(page)` — Ctrl+Space and wait for suggest widget\n114\t  - `goToDefinition(page)` — F12 or Ctrl+Click\n115\t  - `findReferences(page)` — Shift+F12\n116\t  - `rename(page, newName)` — F2\n117\t  - `getCodeActions(page)` — Ctrl+. and wait for lightbulb menu\n118\t  - `waitForDiagnostics(page)` — wait for squiggly underlines to appear\n119\t- [ ] Write `helpers/capture.ts` — screenshot with optional bounding-box highlight and caption\n120\t- [ ] Write a smoke test: open code-server, load demo workspace, verify smelt extension activates (look for diagnostics on a broken file)\n121\t\n122\t**Verification**:\n123\t- [ ] `cd docs/demos && npx playwright test tests/smoke.spec.ts` passes\n124\t- [ ] code-server launches with smelt extension loaded\n125\t- [ ] Screenshot of activated extension saved to `media/smoke.png`\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## Phase 1: Real-Time Diagnostics Demo `[ ]`\n130\t\n131\t**Goal**: Showcase how smelt catches errors as you type — the most immediately compelling LSP feature.\n132\t\n133\t**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer creates a new model referencing an upstream model, but makes a typo. The error appears instantly with a descriptive message. They also see type mismatches caught across model boundaries.\n134\t\n135\t**Demo sequence** (3 screenshots + 1 short video):\n136\t\n137\t1. **Screenshot: \"Clean pipeline\"** — Open `user_lifetime_value.sql`, show no errors. Caption: *\"A healthy pipeline — all references resolve, all types check out.\"*\n138\t\n139\t2. **Video: \"Typo caught instantly\"** — Open `bad_ref.sql` which has `smelt.ref('stg_uusers')` (typo). Show:\n140\t   - File opens with red squiggly under `'stg_uusers'`\n141\t   - Hover over the error → tooltip shows \"Undefined model reference: stg_uusers\"\n142\t   - Fix the typo to `'stg_users'` → squiggly disappears in real-time\n143\t   - Caption: *\"Catch model reference typos before they hit production.\"*\n144\t\n145\t3. **Screenshot: \"Type mismatch across models\"** — Open `type_mismatch.sql` which compares `user_id` (INTEGER) with a string literal `'abc'`. Show:\n146\t   - Yellow/red squiggly on the comparison\n147\t   - Hover tooltip showing the type mismatch details\n148\t   - Caption: *\"Cross-model type checking catches mismatches that dbt can't.\"*\n149\t\n150\t4. **Screenshot: \"Undeclared column\"** — Open `missing_column.sql` which selects `nonexistent_col` from a model. Show:\n151\t   - Error squiggly on the column reference\n152\t   - Caption: *\"Schema-aware diagnostics know exactly which columns exist upstream.\"*\n153\t\n154\t**Test file**: `tests/diagnostics.spec.ts`\n155\t\n156\t**Verification**:\n157\t- [ ] 3 screenshots and 1 video in `media/diagnostics/`\n158\t- [ ] Each screenshot clearly shows the diagnostic with readable text\n159\t\n160\t---\n161\t\n162\t## Phase 2: Go-to-Definition Demo `[ ]`\n163\t\n164\t**Goal**: Show how Ctrl+Click navigation works across the model dependency graph — the feature that makes exploring a data pipeline feel like navigating a real codebase.\n165\t\n166\t**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is debugging a revenue discrepancy in `daily_revenue.sql`. They need to trace the calculation upstream through the model chain to find where `revenue_cents` is defined and transformed.\n167\t\n168\t**Demo sequence** (1 video + 2 screenshots):\n169\t\n170\t1. **Video: \"Trace a column through the pipeline\"** — Start in `daily_revenue.sql`:\n171\t   - Ctrl+Click on `smelt.ref('user_first_purchase')` → jumps to `user_first_purchase.sql`\n172\t   - Ctrl+Click on `smelt.ref('stg_events')` → jumps to `stg_events.sql`\n173\t   - Ctrl+Click on `smelt.source('raw.events')` → jumps to `sources.yml` at the events table definition\n174\t   - Caption: *\"Three clicks to trace a metric from dashboard to raw source.\"*\n175\t\n176\t2. **Screenshot: \"Jump to CTE definition\"** — In `user_sessions.sql` (which uses a WITH clause), Ctrl+Click on a CTE reference in the final SELECT → cursor jumps to the CTE definition. Caption: *\"Navigate within complex queries too — CTE definitions are one click away.\"*\n177\t\n178\t3. **Screenshot: \"Jump to source definition\"** — Show the cursor landing in `sources.yml` with the exact table highlighted. Caption: *\"Source definitions in YAML are first-class navigation targets.\"*\n179\t\n180\t**Test file**: `tests/goto-definition.spec.ts`\n181\t\n182\t**Verification**:\n183\t- [ ] 1 video and 2 screenshots in `media/goto-definition/`\n184\t- [ ] Video clearly shows the file tab changing with each jump\n185\t\n186\t---\n187\t\n188\t## Phase 3: Hover Information Demo `[ ]`\n189\t\n190\t**Goal**: Show the rich schema information available on hover — instant documentation without leaving your editor.\n191\t\n192\t**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is writing a new mart model and needs to know what columns and types are available from upstream models, without switching files.\n193\t\n194\t**Demo sequence** (3 screenshots):\n195\t\n196\t1. **Screenshot: \"Model schema on hover\"** — In `user_lifetime_value.sql`, hover over `smelt.ref('user_first_purchase')`. Show the hover popup with the full schema: column names, types, nullability. Caption: *\"Hover any model reference to see its full schema — no file switching needed.\"*\n197\t\n198\t2. **Screenshot: \"Column type on hover\"** — Hover over a column name like `revenue_cents` in a SELECT. Show type information (INTEGER). Caption: *\"Every column carries its type — hover to inspect.\"*\n199\t\n200\t3. **Screenshot: \"Source schema on hover\"** — Hover over `smelt.source('raw.events')`. Show the source's declared columns and types from `sources.yml`. Caption: *\"Source schemas from YAML are surfaced directly in your SQL.\"*\n201\t\n202\t**Test file**: `tests/hover.spec.ts`\n203\t\n204\t**Verification**:\n205\t- [ ] 3 screenshots in `media/hover/`\n206\t- [ ] Hover popups are fully visible and readable\n207\t\n208\t---\n209\t\n210\t## Phase 4: Code Completion Demo `[ ]`\n211\t\n212\t**Goal**: Show intelligent, context-aware completions that know your data model — not just SQL keywords.\n213\t\n214\t**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is building a new model from scratch. Completions help them discover available models, columns, and write correct SQL without memorizing the schema.\n215\t\n216\t**Demo sequence** (1 video + 2 screenshots):\n217\t\n218\t1. **Video: \"Build a query with completions\"** — Create a new empty model file, then:\n219\t   - Type `SELECT ` then trigger completion → see column names from context\n220\t   - Type `FROM smelt.ref('` → see all available model names as completions\n221\t   - Select `stg_users` from the list\n222\t   - On the next line, type a column name prefix → see matching columns from `stg_users` schema\n223\t   - Caption: *\"Schema-aware completions guide you through the entire query.\"*\n224\t\n225\t2. **Screenshot: \"Model name completions\"** — Inside `ref('`, show the completion dropdown listing all available models with their paths. Caption: *\"Discover models by name — no need to browse the file tree.\"*\n226\t\n227\t3. **Screenshot: \"Column completions from upstream\"** — After a FROM clause referencing a model, trigger completion in SELECT to show columns pulled from that model's schema. Caption: *\"Completions know the upstream schema — you get the right columns, not just SQL keywords.\"*\n228\t\n229\t**Test file**: `tests/completion.spec.ts`\n230\t\n231\t**Verification**:\n232\t- [ ] 1 video and 2 screenshots in `media/completion/`\n233\t- [ ] Completion dropdown is clearly visible with model/column names\n234\t\n235\t---\n236\t\n237\t## Phase 5: Find References Demo `[ ]`\n238\t\n239\t**Goal**: Show how to answer \"who depends on this model?\" — essential for impact analysis before making changes.\n240\t\n241\t**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer needs to change the schema of `stg_events` (renaming a column). Before doing so, they need to know every downstream model that would be affected.\n242\t\n243\t**Demo sequence** (2 screenshots):\n244\t\n245\t1. **Screenshot: \"Find all consumers of a model\"** — In `stg_events.sql`, trigger Find References on the model name. Show the references panel listing `user_sessions.sql`, `user_first_purchase.sql`, and `daily_revenue.sql` (all models that `ref('stg_events')`). Caption: *\"Before changing a model, see every downstream consumer — instant impact analysis.\"*\n246\t\n247\t2. **Screenshot: \"Find CTE references within a file\"** — In a model with CTEs, find references on a CTE name. Show all uses within the file highlighted. Caption: *\"Works for CTEs too — see every use of a subquery within complex models.\"*\n248\t\n249\t**Test file**: `tests/references.spec.ts`\n250\t\n251\t**Verification**:\n252\t- [ ] 2 screenshots in `media/references/`\n253\t- [ ] References panel is visible with file paths and line numbers\n254\t\n255\t---\n256\t\n257\t## Phase 6: Rename Refactoring Demo `[ ]`\n258\t\n259\t**Goal**: Show safe, project-wide renames — the feature that turns a scary find-and-replace into a confident one-step operation.\n260\t\n261\t**Motivating scenario**: The team decides to rename `stg_events` to `stg_activity_events` for clarity. In dbt, this means manually updating every `ref('stg_events')` across the project and hoping you didn't miss one. In smelt, it's one F2.\n262\t\n263\t**Demo sequence** (1 video + 1 screenshot):\n264\t\n265\t1. **Video: \"Rename a model across the project\"** — In `stg_events.sql`:\n266\t   - Place cursor on the model name\n267\t   - Press F2 → rename dialog appears\n268\t   - Type `stg_activity_events`\n269\t   - Press Enter → show all `ref('stg_events')` calls updating across multiple files simultaneously\n270\t   - Show the file being renamed too\n271\t   - Caption: *\"One keystroke renames the model and updates every reference across the project.\"*\n272\t\n273\t2. **Screenshot: \"Rename preview\"** — Show the rename preview (if VS Code shows one) with all affected files listed. Caption: *\"See every change before it happens — no surprises.\"*\n274\t\n275\t**Test file**: `tests/rename.spec.ts`\n276\t\n277\t**Verification**:\n278\t- [ ] 1 video and 1 screenshot in `media/rename/`\n279\t- [ ] Video clearly shows multiple files updating\n280\t\n281\t---\n282\t\n283\t## Phase 7: Code Actions / Quick Fixes Demo `[ ]`\n284\t\n285\t**Goal**: Show intelligent quick fixes that don't just report problems but offer solutions.\n286\t\n287\t**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is iterating quickly — they reference a model that doesn't exist yet, compare mismatched types, and reference a source table not in `sources.yml`. Each time, the lightbulb offers a fix.\n288\t\n289\t**Demo sequence** (3 screenshots + 1 video):\n290\t\n291\t1. **Video: \"Create a model from a reference\"** — Write a new model with `smelt.ref('user_churn')` which doesn't exist:\n292\t   - Red squiggly appears under the ref\n293\t   - Click the lightbulb (or Ctrl+.) → \"Create model 'user_churn'\" appears\n294\t   - Select it → a new `user_churn.sql` file is created with a template\n295\t   - Caption: *\"Reference a model before it exists — smelt scaffolds it for you.\"*\n296\t\n297\t2. **Screenshot: \"Fix type mismatch with CAST\"** — Show a type mismatch diagnostic with the lightbulb offering `CAST(column AS INTEGER)`. Caption: *\"Type mismatches come with a one-click CAST fix.\"*\n298\t\n299\t3. **Screenshot: \"Add missing source\"** — Show an undefined source diagnostic with the lightbulb offering to add the source to `sources.yml`. Caption: *\"Missing source? The quick fix adds it to your YAML config.\"*\n300\t\n301\t4. **Screenshot: \"Add missing column to source\"** — Show an undeclared column on a source with the lightbulb offering to add the column definition. Caption: *\"Even column declarations can be auto-added to your source definitions.\"*\n302\t\n303\t**Test file**: `tests/code-actions.spec.ts`\n304\t\n305\t**Verification**:\n306\t- [ ] 1 video and 3 screenshots in `media/code-actions/`\n307\t- [ ] Lightbulb menu is visible with action descriptions\n308\t\n309\t---\n310\t\n311\t## Phase 8: Documentation Assembly `[ ]`\n312\t\n313\t**Goal**: Assemble all media into polished documentation pages.\n314\t\n315\t**Work items**:\n316\t- [ ] Write `docs/demos/output/lsp-features.md` — main documentation page with all features, screenshots, and video embeds\n317\t- [ ] Write `docs/demos/output/getting-started.md` — quick-start guide with the most impressive 3 screenshots\n318\t- [ ] Create a `docs/demos/generate-docs.ts` script that:\n319\t  - Scans `media/` for generated assets\n320\t  - Generates markdown with proper image/video references\n321\t  - Adds captions and feature descriptions\n322\t  - Outputs to `output/`\n323\t- [ ] Add a comparison section: \"smelt vs dbt\" showing what each feature replaces (manual grep, prayer, etc.)\n324\t- [ ] Update the main `README.md` with a \"Editor Features\" section linking to the full docs\n325\t- [ ] Optimize media: compress PNGs, convert videos to GIF where short enough, ensure reasonable file sizes\n326\t\n327\t**Verification**:\n328\t- [ ] `docs/demos/output/lsp-features.md` renders correctly with all media\n329\t- [ ] Total media size is under 20MB\n330\t- [ ] All image/video links resolve\n331\t\n332\t---\n333\t\n334\t## Phase 9: CI Integration `[ ]`\n335\t\n336\t**Goal**: Make the demos reproducible and keep them from going stale as features evolve.\n337\t\n338\t**Work items**:\n339\t- [ ] Add a `docs/demos/run-all.sh` script that:\n340\t  1. Builds the smelt-lsp binary\n341\t  2. Packages the VS Code extension\n342\t  3. Starts code-server with the extension\n343\t  4. Runs all Playwright tests (which generate media)\n344\t  5. Runs the doc generation script\n345\t  6. Shuts down code-server\n346\t- [ ] Add instructions to `docs/demos/README.md` for running locally\n347\t- [ ] Consider a GitHub Actions workflow that regenerates demos on LSP changes (optional — may defer)\n348\t- [ ] Add a `--update-snapshots` flag for refreshing media after intentional UI changes\n349\t\n350\t**Verification**:\n351\t- [ ] `docs/demos/run-all.sh` runs end-to-end and produces all media + docs\n352\t- [ ] Clean run from scratch works (no leftover state dependencies)\n353\t\n354\t---\n355\t\n356\t## Session Log\n357\t\n358\t*(Entries added by the automation loop after each session)*\n359\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05b1b068-6028-4f68-a58e-10ea723d627b","uuid":"68a4b4b1-2ff8-4f41-9b8c-4a4aad0ac01a","timestamp":"2026-04-06T04:43:10.600Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/andrew/smelt-sql/.claude/worktrees/lspdemo/docs/plans/20260406-lsp-visual-docs.md","content":"# Plan: LSP Visual Documentation with Playwright Demos\n\n**Date**: 2026-04-06\n**Branch**: `lsp-visual-docs`\n**Script**: `scripts/run-lsp-visual-docs-loop.sh`\n\n## Design Principle\n\n**Show, don't tell.** Each LSP feature gets a motivating real-world scenario that a data engineer would actually encounter, automated via Playwright against VS Code Server (code-server) to produce screenshots and screen recordings. Documentation is generated as markdown with embedded media, suitable for the project README, docs site, or GitHub wiki.\n\n## Demo Infrastructure\n\n**Approach**: Playwright drives a headless (or headed) code-server instance with the smelt extension installed. Each demo script:\n1. Opens a purpose-built example workspace with a compelling scenario\n2. Performs editor interactions (typing, hovering, clicking, Ctrl+click, etc.)\n3. Captures annotated screenshots and/or short video clips\n4. Outputs media to `docs/demos/media/`\n\n**Why code-server**: It's VS Code in a browser — Playwright's native territory. No Electron hacks, no flaky desktop automation. The smelt extension loads identically since it's a standard LSP client.\n\n**Demo workspace**: `examples/demo_workspace/` — a small but realistic analytics pipeline designed so each feature has a natural, compelling demonstration point.\n\n## Demo Workspace Design\n\nThe workspace tells a story: a startup's analytics pipeline with users, events, and revenue.\n\n```\nexamples/demo_workspace/\n├── smelt.yml\n├── sources.yml                    # raw data sources with typed columns\n├── models/\n│   ├── staging/\n│   │   ├── stg_users.sql          # clean user data from source\n│   │   └── stg_events.sql         # clean event data from source  \n│   ├── intermediate/\n│   │   ├── user_sessions.sql      # sessionized events (refs stg_events)\n│   │   └── user_first_purchase.sql # first purchase per user (refs stg_events, stg_users)\n│   └── marts/\n│       ├── user_lifetime_value.sql # LTV calculation (refs multiple intermediates)\n│       └── daily_revenue.sql       # daily revenue rollup\n└── models_broken/                  # copies with intentional errors for demo\n    ├── bad_ref.sql                 # typo in smelt.ref() → diagnostics demo\n    ├── type_mismatch.sql           # VARCHAR vs INTEGER comparison → type error demo\n    └── missing_column.sql          # references non-existent column → undeclared column demo\n```\n\n## Status Key\n\n- `[ ]` — Not started\n- `[~]` — In progress\n- `[x]` — Complete\n- `[!]` — Blocked or needs review\n\n---\n\n## Phase 0: Demo Infrastructure Setup `[~]`\n\n**Goal**: Set up Playwright, code-server, and the demo workspace so all subsequent phases can focus purely on scripting demos.\n\n**Work items**:\n- [ ] Create `docs/demos/` directory structure:\n  ```\n  docs/demos/\n  ├── package.json              # Playwright + dependencies\n  ├── playwright.config.ts      # code-server base URL, timeouts, video settings\n  ├── helpers/\n  │   ├── code-server.ts        # Launch/teardown code-server with smelt extension\n  │   ├── editor.ts             # High-level helpers: openFile(), typeText(), hover(), triggerCompletion(), goToDefinition(), etc.\n  │   └── capture.ts            # Screenshot/video capture with annotation overlays\n  ├── scripts/\n  │   └── start-code-server.sh  # Launch code-server pointing at demo workspace\n  ├── tests/                    # Playwright test files (one per feature)\n  ├── media/                    # Generated screenshots and videos\n  └── output/                   # Generated markdown documentation\n  ```\n- [ ] Create `examples/demo_workspace/` with `smelt.yml` and `sources.yml`\n- [ ] Write `sources.yml` with a realistic schema:\n  ```yaml\n  sources:\n    raw:\n      tables:\n        users:\n          columns:\n            - name: user_id\n              type: INTEGER\n            - name: email\n              type: VARCHAR\n            - name: signup_date\n              type: DATE\n            - name: plan_type\n              type: VARCHAR\n        events:\n          columns:\n            - name: event_id\n              type: INTEGER\n            - name: user_id\n              type: INTEGER\n            - name: event_type\n              type: VARCHAR\n            - name: event_time\n              type: TIMESTAMP\n            - name: revenue_cents\n              type: INTEGER\n  ```\n- [ ] Write all model SQL files (staging, intermediate, marts) — keep them short (5-15 lines each) but realistic\n- [ ] Write the `models_broken/` variants with intentional errors\n- [ ] Install Playwright: `npm init -y && npm install @playwright/test playwright`\n- [ ] Write `helpers/code-server.ts` — launches code-server with `--install-extension` for the smelt VSIX, returns the base URL\n- [ ] Write `helpers/editor.ts` — wraps common VS Code web interactions:\n  - `openFile(page, path)` — via quick-open (Ctrl+P)\n  - `goToLine(page, line)` — via Ctrl+G\n  - `hoverWord(page, word)` — position cursor and wait for hover widget\n  - `triggerCompletion(page)` — Ctrl+Space and wait for suggest widget\n  - `goToDefinition(page)` — F12 or Ctrl+Click\n  - `findReferences(page)` — Shift+F12\n  - `rename(page, newName)` — F2\n  - `getCodeActions(page)` — Ctrl+. and wait for lightbulb menu\n  - `waitForDiagnostics(page)` — wait for squiggly underlines to appear\n- [ ] Write `helpers/capture.ts` — screenshot with optional bounding-box highlight and caption\n- [ ] Write a smoke test: open code-server, load demo workspace, verify smelt extension activates (look for diagnostics on a broken file)\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] `cd docs/demos && npx playwright test tests/smoke.spec.ts` passes\n- [ ] code-server launches with smelt extension loaded\n- [ ] Screenshot of activated extension saved to `media/smoke.png`\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Real-Time Diagnostics Demo `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Showcase how smelt catches errors as you type — the most immediately compelling LSP feature.\n\n**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer creates a new model referencing an upstream model, but makes a typo. The error appears instantly with a descriptive message. They also see type mismatches caught across model boundaries.\n\n**Demo sequence** (3 screenshots + 1 short video):\n\n1. **Screenshot: \"Clean pipeline\"** — Open `user_lifetime_value.sql`, show no errors. Caption: *\"A healthy pipeline — all references resolve, all types check out.\"*\n\n2. **Video: \"Typo caught instantly\"** — Open `bad_ref.sql` which has `smelt.ref('stg_uusers')` (typo). Show:\n   - File opens with red squiggly under `'stg_uusers'`\n   - Hover over the error → tooltip shows \"Undefined model reference: stg_uusers\"\n   - Fix the typo to `'stg_users'` → squiggly disappears in real-time\n   - Caption: *\"Catch model reference typos before they hit production.\"*\n\n3. **Screenshot: \"Type mismatch across models\"** — Open `type_mismatch.sql` which compares `user_id` (INTEGER) with a string literal `'abc'`. Show:\n   - Yellow/red squiggly on the comparison\n   - Hover tooltip showing the type mismatch details\n   - Caption: *\"Cross-model type checking catches mismatches that dbt can't.\"*\n\n4. **Screenshot: \"Undeclared column\"** — Open `missing_column.sql` which selects `nonexistent_col` from a model. Show:\n   - Error squiggly on the column reference\n   - Caption: *\"Schema-aware diagnostics know exactly which columns exist upstream.\"*\n\n**Test file**: `tests/diagnostics.spec.ts`\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] 3 screenshots and 1 video in `media/diagnostics/`\n- [ ] Each screenshot clearly shows the diagnostic with readable text\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Go-to-Definition Demo `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Show how Ctrl+Click navigation works across the model dependency graph — the feature that makes exploring a data pipeline feel like navigating a real codebase.\n\n**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is debugging a revenue discrepancy in `daily_revenue.sql`. They need to trace the calculation upstream through the model chain to find where `revenue_cents` is defined and transformed.\n\n**Demo sequence** (1 video + 2 screenshots):\n\n1. **Video: \"Trace a column through the pipeline\"** — Start in `daily_revenue.sql`:\n   - Ctrl+Click on `smelt.ref('user_first_purchase')` → jumps to `user_first_purchase.sql`\n   - Ctrl+Click on `smelt.ref('stg_events')` → jumps to `stg_events.sql`\n   - Ctrl+Click on `smelt.source('raw.events')` → jumps to `sources.yml` at the events table definition\n   - Caption: *\"Three clicks to trace a metric from dashboard to raw source.\"*\n\n2. **Screenshot: \"Jump to CTE definition\"** — In `user_sessions.sql` (which uses a WITH clause), Ctrl+Click on a CTE reference in the final SELECT → cursor jumps to the CTE definition. Caption: *\"Navigate within complex queries too — CTE definitions are one click away.\"*\n\n3. **Screenshot: \"Jump to source definition\"** — Show the cursor landing in `sources.yml` with the exact table highlighted. Caption: *\"Source definitions in YAML are first-class navigation targets.\"*\n\n**Test file**: `tests/goto-definition.spec.ts`\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] 1 video and 2 screenshots in `media/goto-definition/`\n- [ ] Video clearly shows the file tab changing with each jump\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Hover Information Demo `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Show the rich schema information available on hover — instant documentation without leaving your editor.\n\n**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is writing a new mart model and needs to know what columns and types are available from upstream models, without switching files.\n\n**Demo sequence** (3 screenshots):\n\n1. **Screenshot: \"Model schema on hover\"** — In `user_lifetime_value.sql`, hover over `smelt.ref('user_first_purchase')`. Show the hover popup with the full schema: column names, types, nullability. Caption: *\"Hover any model reference to see its full schema — no file switching needed.\"*\n\n2. **Screenshot: \"Column type on hover\"** — Hover over a column name like `revenue_cents` in a SELECT. Show type information (INTEGER). Caption: *\"Every column carries its type — hover to inspect.\"*\n\n3. **Screenshot: \"Source schema on hover\"** — Hover over `smelt.source('raw.events')`. Show the source's declared columns and types from `sources.yml`. Caption: *\"Source schemas from YAML are surfaced directly in your SQL.\"*\n\n**Test file**: `tests/hover.spec.ts`\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] 3 screenshots in `media/hover/`\n- [ ] Hover popups are fully visible and readable\n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Code Completion Demo `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Show intelligent, context-aware completions that know your data model — not just SQL keywords.\n\n**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is building a new model from scratch. Completions help them discover available models, columns, and write correct SQL without memorizing the schema.\n\n**Demo sequence** (1 video + 2 screenshots):\n\n1. **Video: \"Build a query with completions\"** — Create a new empty model file, then:\n   - Type `SELECT ` then trigger completion → see column names from context\n   - Type `FROM smelt.ref('` → see all available model names as completions\n   - Select `stg_users` from the list\n   - On the next line, type a column name prefix → see matching columns from `stg_users` schema\n   - Caption: *\"Schema-aware completions guide you through the entire query.\"*\n\n2. **Screenshot: \"Model name completions\"** — Inside `ref('`, show the completion dropdown listing all available models with their paths. Caption: *\"Discover models by name — no need to browse the file tree.\"*\n\n3. **Screenshot: \"Column completions from upstream\"** — After a FROM clause referencing a model, trigger completion in SELECT to show columns pulled from that model's schema. Caption: *\"Completions know the upstream schema — you get the right columns, not just SQL keywords.\"*\n\n**Test file**: `tests/completion.spec.ts`\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] 1 video and 2 screenshots in `media/completion/`\n- [ ] Completion dropdown is clearly visible with model/column names\n\n---\n\n## Phase 5: Find References Demo `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Show how to answer \"who depends on this model?\" — essential for impact analysis before making changes.\n\n**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer needs to change the schema of `stg_events` (renaming a column). Before doing so, they need to know every downstream model that would be affected.\n\n**Demo sequence** (2 screenshots):\n\n1. **Screenshot: \"Find all consumers of a model\"** — In `stg_events.sql`, trigger Find References on the model name. Show the references panel listing `user_sessions.sql`, `user_first_purchase.sql`, and `daily_revenue.sql` (all models that `ref('stg_events')`). Caption: *\"Before changing a model, see every downstream consumer — instant impact analysis.\"*\n\n2. **Screenshot: \"Find CTE references within a file\"** — In a model with CTEs, find references on a CTE name. Show all uses within the file highlighted. Caption: *\"Works for CTEs too — see every use of a subquery within complex models.\"*\n\n**Test file**: `tests/references.spec.ts`\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] 2 screenshots in `media/references/`\n- [ ] References panel is visible with file paths and line numbers\n\n---\n\n## Phase 6: Rename Refactoring Demo `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Show safe, project-wide renames — the feature that turns a scary find-and-replace into a confident one-step operation.\n\n**Motivating scenario**: The team decides to rename `stg_events` to `stg_activity_events` for clarity. In dbt, this means manually updating every `ref('stg_events')` across the project and hoping you didn't miss one. In smelt, it's one F2.\n\n**Demo sequence** (1 video + 1 screenshot):\n\n1. **Video: \"Rename a model across the project\"** — In `stg_events.sql`:\n   - Place cursor on the model name\n   - Press F2 → rename dialog appears\n   - Type `stg_activity_events`\n   - Press Enter → show all `ref('stg_events')` calls updating across multiple files simultaneously\n   - Show the file being renamed too\n   - Caption: *\"One keystroke renames the model and updates every reference across the project.\"*\n\n2. **Screenshot: \"Rename preview\"** — Show the rename preview (if VS Code shows one) with all affected files listed. Caption: *\"See every change before it happens — no surprises.\"*\n\n**Test file**: `tests/rename.spec.ts`\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] 1 video and 1 screenshot in `media/rename/`\n- [ ] Video clearly shows multiple files updating\n\n---\n\n## Phase 7: Code Actions / Quick Fixes Demo `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Show intelligent quick fixes that don't just report problems but offer solutions.\n\n**Motivating scenario**: A data engineer is iterating quickly — they reference a model that doesn't exist yet, compare mismatched types, and reference a source table not in `sources.yml`. Each time, the lightbulb offers a fix.\n\n**Demo sequence** (3 screenshots + 1 video):\n\n1. **Video: \"Create a model from a reference\"** — Write a new model with `smelt.ref('user_churn')` which doesn't exist:\n   - Red squiggly appears under the ref\n   - Click the lightbulb (or Ctrl+.) → \"Create model 'user_churn'\" appears\n   - Select it → a new `user_churn.sql` file is created with a template\n   - Caption: *\"Reference a model before it exists — smelt scaffolds it for you.\"*\n\n2. **Screenshot: \"Fix type mismatch with CAST\"** — Show a type mismatch diagnostic with the lightbulb offering `CAST(column AS INTEGER)`. Caption: *\"Type mismatches come with a one-click CAST fix.\"*\n\n3. **Screenshot: \"Add missing source\"** — Show an undefined source diagnostic with the lightbulb offering to add the source to `sources.yml`. Caption: *\"Missing source? The quick fix adds it to your YAML config.\"*\n\n4. **Screenshot: \"Add missing column to source\"** — Show an undeclared column on a source with the lightbulb offering to add the column definition. Caption: *\"Even column declarations can be auto-added to your source definitions.\"*\n\n**Test file**: `tests/code-actions.spec.ts`\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] 1 video and 3 screenshots in `media/code-actions/`\n- [ ] Lightbulb menu is visible with action descriptions\n\n---\n\n## Phase 8: Documentation Assembly `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Assemble all media into polished documentation pages.\n\n**Work items**:\n- [ ] Write `docs/demos/output/lsp-features.md` — main documentation page with all features, screenshots, and video embeds\n- [ ] Write `docs/demos/output/getting-started.md` — quick-start guide with the most impressive 3 screenshots\n- [ ] Create a `docs/demos/generate-docs.ts` script that:\n  - Scans `media/` for generated assets\n  - Generates markdown with proper image/video references\n  - Adds captions and feature descriptions\n  - Outputs to `output/`\n- [ ] Add a comparison section: \"smelt vs dbt\" showing what each feature replaces (manual grep, prayer, etc.)\n- [ ] Update the main `README.md` with a \"Editor Features\" section linking to the full docs\n- [ ] Optimize media: compress PNGs, convert videos to GIF where short enough, ensure reasonable file sizes\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] `docs/demos/output/lsp-features.md` renders correctly with all media\n- [ ] Total media size is under 20MB\n- [ ] All image/video links resolve\n\n---\n\n## Phase 9: CI Integration `[ ]`\n\n**Goal**: Make the demos reproducible and keep them from going stale as features evolve.\n\n**Work items**:\n- [ ] Add a `docs/demos/run-all.sh` script that:\n  1. Builds the smelt-lsp binary\n  2. Packages the VS Code extension\n  3. Starts code-server with the extension\n  4. Runs all Playwright tests (which generate media)\n  5. Runs the doc generation script\n  6. Shuts down code-server\n- [ ] Add instructions to `docs/demos/README.md` for running locally\n- [ ] Consider a GitHub Actions workflow that regenerates demos on LSP changes (optional — may defer)\n- [ ] Add a `--update-snapshots` flag for refreshing media after intentional UI changes\n\n**Verification**:\n- [ ] `docs/demos/run-all.sh` runs end-to-end and produces all media + docs\n- [ ] Clean run from scratch works (no leftover state dependencies)\n\n---\n\n## Session Log\n\n*(Entries added by the automation loop after each session)*\n","numLines":359,"startLine":1,"totalLines":359}}}
